function renderMethods(){return heading('methods')+`<div class="method-grid"><section class="card"><h3>What NISP means</h3><p>Number of identified specimens (NISP) records fragments/specimens attributed to a taxonomic category. It does not directly estimate numbers of animals. Fragmentation and recording intensity can distort comparisons.</p></section><section class="card"><h3>Pooling vs equal assemblage weight</h3><p><strong>Pooled:</strong> sum taxon NISP across units before calculating a percentage. <strong>Site/assemblage mean:</strong> compute a percentage for each unit with a valid denominator and take the unweighted arithmetic mean. Neither is always preferable.</p></section><section class="card"><h3>Denominator transparency</h3><p>“All” divides by total recorded NISP across taxa in the filtered dataset. “Selected” divides only by the included taxa. This changes your archaeological question and should be stated when publishing.</p></section><section class="card"><h3>Species ratios</h3><p>Percentage share A/(A+B) is bounded from 0–100 and remains defined when B=0 but A&gt;0. A/B is undefined when B=0. The app does not allocate Ovis/Capra fragments.</p></section><section class="card"><h3>Comparative datasets</h3><p>Carefully compare excavation methods, recovery practices, preservation, osteological criteria, taxonomic scope, site functions, and sample size. Country and region labels are standardised only by the imported metadata.</p></section><section class="card"><h3>Chronology</h3><p>Period names are ordered using the editable list. A line plot connects categorical periods and does not claim that each period covers the same duration. Periods spanning multiple phases are never automatically split.</p></section><section class="card"><h3>Missing vs zero</h3><p>Unreported species cannot reliably be distinguished from reported zero counts unless an explicit 0 row is provided. In comparisons, absent labels are treated as 0 of the recorded composition; validate table completeness before inference.</p></section><section class="card"><h3>Provenance & limitations</h3><p>Keep bibliographic/source, context, collection and chronology metadata. The initial version is descriptive: no formal tests of significance, sample-size correction, rarefaction, confidence intervals or MNI are implied.</p></section></div><div class="card card-pad" style="margin-top:18px"><h3 class="card-title">Supported input</h3><p class="small-copy">CSV (.csv, including semicolon/tab-separated) or XLSX (first worksheet, ordinary numeric/text values; no formulas evaluated). Both long format (one taxon per row) and wide format (one taxon per column). Required: site, taxon and NISP; for wide format: site and at least one NISP taxon column. Country, region, period, context, assemblage ID, source, dates and coordinates are optional. All analysis occurs locally. Data are persisted in IndexedDB where available.</p></div>`;}

